# Protocol Cheatsheet

# 1. Gemini

Gemini is a request-response protocol on top of TLS. The client opens a connection and sends a single request line. The server sends back a response header, optionally followed by a body, and then closes the connection.

=> gemini://geminiprotocol.net/docs/protocol-specification.gmi  Gemini protocol specification
=> gemini://geminiprotocol.net/docs/gemtext-specification.gmi  Gemtext specification

## 1.1 Connection

* The default port is 1965.
* TLS 1.2 or newer is required, and the client must use SNI.
* Server certificates are usually self-signed. Clients verify them with TOFU (Trust On First Use): the certificate fingerprint seen on the first visit is remembered and later connections are checked against it. Lagrange stores the fingerprints in "trusted.lst" (see the Reference, section 1.2).
* A client certificate is only sent when the user has chosen to use one. Servers ask for one with a 6x status code.

## 1.2 Request

```Gemini request
<URL><CR><LF>
```

* The URL must be absolute and encoded in UTF-8. It may be at most 1024 bytes long.
* The URL must not contain a userinfo part or a fragment.
* User input is sent as the query string, percent-encoded (for example, spaces as "%20").
* Requests have no body. Use Titan to upload data (see section 2).

## 1.3 Response

```Gemini response
<STATUS><SPACE><META><CR><LF>
[<BODY>]
```

* STATUS is a two-digit code. The first digit is the category. An unknown code is handled like the "x0" code of its category.
* META is at most 1024 bytes of UTF-8. Its meaning depends on the status code.
* Only successful (2x) responses have a body.
* In a 20 response, META is the media type of the body, with optional "charset" and "lang" parameters. An empty META means "text/gemini; charset=utf-8".

## 1.4 Status codes

```Gemini status codes (table)
10  Input                        META: prompt for the user
11  Sensitive input              META: prompt; input is not echoed
20  Success                      META: media type of the body
30  Temporary redirect           META: new URL (may be relative)
31  Permanent redirect           META: new URL (may be relative)
40  Temporary failure
41  Server unavailable
42  CGI error
43  Proxy error
44  Slow down
50  Permanent failure
51  Not found
52  Gone
53  Proxy request refused
59  Bad request
60  Client certificate required
61  Certificate not authorized
62  Certificate not valid
```

In 4x, 5x, and 6x responses, META may contain an error message to show to the user.

## 1.5 Examples

Fetching a page:
```Example of a successful request (C: client, S: server)
C: gemini://example.com/<CR><LF>
S: 20 text/gemini; lang=en<CR><LF>
S: # Example Capsule
S: Welcome to my capsule!
```

Asking for input:
```Example of an input request (C: client, S: server)
C: gemini://example.com/search<CR><LF>
S: 10 Enter search terms<CR><LF>
   (the user enters "gemini clients")
C: gemini://example.com/search?gemini%20clients<CR><LF>
S: 20 text/gemini<CR><LF>
S: ...
```

Making a request on the command line with OpenSSL:
```
printf 'gemini://geminiprotocol.net/\r\n' | openssl s_client -quiet -connect geminiprotocol.net:1965 -servername geminiprotocol.net
```

# 2. Titan

Titan is a sister protocol of Gemini for uploading data to a server. Connections work the same way as in Gemini, so the same server and client certificates can be used. The request line is followed by a payload of arbitrary size, and the server replies with a Gemini response.

=> gemini://transjovian.org/titan  Titan protocol (by Alex Schroeder)

## 2.1 Request

```Titan request
titan://<host>[:<port>]/<path>;size=<N>[;mime=<type>][;token=<token>]<CR><LF>
<payload of N bytes>
```

Parameters are appended to the URL path, separated with ";":

* "size" (required) is the number of bytes in the payload. A size of zero may be interpreted by the server as a request to delete the resource.
* "mime" (optional) is the media type of the payload. The default is "text/gemini".
* "token" (optional) is a percent-encoded passphrase or command whose meaning is defined by the server.

Servers commonly accept Titan requests on the same port as Gemini (1965). In Lagrange, the port can be changed for each site in the upload dialog.

## 2.2 Response

The response is a Gemini response header (see section 1.3). After a successful upload, servers typically reply with a 30/31 redirect to the "gemini" URL of the uploaded resource, or with a 20 response describing the result.

## 2.3 Edit parameter

When a Titan URL ends with ";edit", Lagrange first sends the URL without a payload, expecting the server to reply with the current contents of the resource in a 20 response. The contents are shown in the upload dialog's text editor, and submitting the dialog uploads the edited version with a normal Titan request.

## 2.4 Examples

Uploading a Gemtext page:
```Example of an upload (C: client, S: server)
C: titan://example.com/notes/todo.gmi;size=27;mime=text/gemini<CR><LF>
C: # To do
C: * Water the plants
S: 30 gemini://example.com/notes/todo.gmi<CR><LF>
```

Uploading an image with a token:
```Example of an upload with a token (C: client, S: server)
C: titan://example.com/images/cat.jpg;size=48211;mime=image/jpeg;token=hunter2<CR><LF>
C: <48211 bytes of JPEG data>
S: 20 text/gemini<CR><LF>
S: Upload complete.
```
